// Guías de salud visual (11/16, Armando: «crear una página útil por cada tema de los ojos que nos falte»): ocho páginas en inglés
// con su traducción (glasses-after-lasik, contacts-vs-glasses, signs-you-need-glasses, how-long-is-a-prescription-valid,
// glasses-for-astigmatism, nearsighted-vs-farsighted, prescription-glasses-cost, how-often-eye-exam). Claves tp.<id>.* (tp.<id>.meta
// lo usa js/pages/tipo.js para el título de la pestaña), gd.* (comunes: lista «Eye care guides», fuentes, aviso final y las cuatro
// preguntas nuevas de ayuda.html, gd.ayuda.*) y rx2.* (receta.html: título, H1 y cada término escrito a la vista).
// receta.docTitle se REDEFINE aquí a propósito: js/pages/receta.js (publicado) escribe document.title con esa clave, y este
// diccionario se carga después de js/i18n/receta.js solo en receta.html (misma clave, mismo significado: el título de esa página).
// Va en un archivo NUEVO porque los diccionarios publicados viven 30 días en el CDN con su ?v= (no se sube ASSET_V).
// Hechos médicos y legales: los de las fuentes que nombra cada página (FDA, AAO, AOA, NEI, FTC, CDC; comprobados el 11/16).
// Precios: NINGUNO aquí; los patrones *.price / *.rN xM llevan huecos que rellena js/pages/guias.js con data/manifest.json.
window.HOE_I18N = window.HOE_I18N || { en: {}, es: {} };
Object.assign(window.HOE_I18N.en, {
  "gd.kicker": "Eye care guide",
  "gd.src": "Sources",
  "gd.disc": "This guide is general information and does not replace an eye exam.",
  "gd.more.kicker": "Guides",
  "gd.more.title": "Eye care guides",
  "gd.cta.rx": "How to read your prescription",
  "gd.n.signs": "Signs you need glasses",
  "gd.n.exam": "How often to get an eye exam",
  "gd.n.rx-valid": "How long a prescription is valid",
  "gd.n.rx": "How to read your prescription",
  "gd.n.near-far": "Nearsighted vs farsighted",
  "gd.n.astig": "Glasses for astigmatism",
  "gd.n.cost": "What prescription glasses cost",
  "gd.n.contacts-vs": "Contact lenses vs glasses",
  "gd.n.lasik": "Glasses after LASIK",
  "tp.lasik.meta": "Do You Still Need Glasses After LASIK? | House of Eyeglasses",
  "tp.lasik.h1": "Do you still need glasses after LASIK?",
  "tp.lasik.lead": "For everyday distance vision, often not. But many people still need glasses for something: LASIK cannot correct presbyopia, so almost everyone with good distance vision needs reading glasses after about 40, and some people are left with a small prescription or with glare at night. Your surgeon is the one to ask about your own eyes.",
  "tp.lasik.r1h": "Reading glasses after 40",
  "tp.lasik.r1p": "<p>LASIK reshapes the cornea, the clear front of the eye. Presbyopia happens inside the eye: with age, the natural lens gets harder and less flexible and stops focusing up close. The American Academy of Ophthalmology (AAO) says LASIK cannot correct presbyopia, and that with or without refractive surgery, almost everyone who has excellent distance vision will need reading glasses after around age 40.</p><p>If you only need help up close, your eye doctor can write a reading prescription, and <a href=\"single-vision-glasses.html\">single vision lenses</a> made with those numbers become reading glasses fitted to each of your eyes. If you need help far and near, <a href=\"progressive-glasses.html\">progressive lenses</a> cover distance, the computer and reading in one pair, with no line.</p>",
  "tp.lasik.r2h": "A small prescription left over",
  "tp.lasik.r2p": "<p>LASIK can leave an eye slightly under- or over-corrected. The U.S. Food and Drug Administration (FDA) lists this among the risks of the surgery and says you may still need glasses or contact lenses afterwards; the AAO adds that these problems often can be improved with glasses, contact lenses or additional laser surgery.</p><p>A prescription written after LASIK is a regular glasses prescription, and we make it like any other, as single vision or progressive lenses, in any frame we carry. A prescription from before the surgery no longer matches your eyes, so don’t use it.</p>",
  "tp.lasik.r3h": "Glare and night driving",
  "tp.lasik.r3p": "<p>Some people see glare, halos or starbursts around lights after LASIK. The AAO says side effects like these usually go away over time but, in rare cases, may not; the FDA says some patients develop glare, halos or double vision that can seriously affect nighttime vision. The AAO also notes that even after LASIK you might need glasses for certain activities, such as reading or driving at night.</p><p>If your eye doctor prescribes glasses for driving at night, an <a href=\"anti-glare-glasses.html\">anti-reflective coating</a> keeps the lenses themselves from adding reflections. It does not treat glare that comes from the eye: that is a question for your surgeon.</p>",
  "tp.lasik.r4h": "Dry eyes and sunglasses",
  "tp.lasik.r4p": "<p>Dry eyes are common after LASIK. The AAO says almost everyone who has it will have dry eyes and changing vision during the day, usually fading within a month, though for some people it takes longer; the FDA notes that some patients develop severe dry eye that may be permanent. Follow your surgeon’s instructions on drops and eye protection while you heal.</p><p>If you still wear a prescription, we can make it as <a href=\"sunglasses.html\">prescription sunglasses</a> with a solid or gradient sun tint, or as <a href=\"photochromic-glasses.html\">light-responsive lenses</a> that darken outdoors. We don’t make sun lenses without a prescription.</p>",
  "tp.lasik.r5h": "Keep up your eye exams",
  "tp.lasik.r5p": "<p>LASIK changes how your eye focuses; it doesn’t replace the checkups that look at the health of your eyes. The American Optometric Association (AOA) says people who have had refractive surgery (LASIK, PRK or SMILE) should still have an eye exam every 1–2 years. More in <a href=\"how-often-eye-exam.html\">how often to get an eye exam</a>.</p>",
  "tp.lasik.src": "<p><a href=\"https://www.fda.gov/medical-devices/lasik/what-are-risks-and-how-can-i-find-right-doctor-me\">U.S. Food and Drug Administration (FDA): LASIK risks</a></p><p><a href=\"https://www.aao.org/eye-health/glasses-contacts/lasik\">American Academy of Ophthalmology (AAO): LASIK</a></p><p><a href=\"https://www.aao.org/eye-health/diseases/what-is-presbyopia\">American Academy of Ophthalmology: What is presbyopia?</a></p><p><a href=\"https://www.nei.nih.gov/eye-health-information/eye-conditions-and-diseases/presbyopia\">National Eye Institute: Presbyopia</a></p><p><a href=\"https://www.aoa.org/healthy-eyes/caring-for-your-eyes/eye-exams\">American Optometric Association (AOA): Comprehensive eye exams</a></p>",
  "tp.lasik.q1": "Will I need reading glasses after LASIK?",
  "tp.lasik.a1": "Most likely, at some point. LASIK cannot correct presbyopia, the age-related loss of close-up focus that shows up in your 40s. Some people choose monovision LASIK, with one eye set for distance and the other slightly nearsighted for close work; the AAO notes it is not for everyone.",
  "tp.lasik.q2": "Can I use my old glasses prescription after LASIK?",
  "tp.lasik.a2": "No. LASIK changes the way your eyes focus, so a prescription written before surgery no longer fits them. If you need glasses afterwards, get a new eye exam and order with the new prescription.",
  "tp.lasik.q3": "How soon after LASIK can I get glasses?",
  "tp.lasik.a3": "Ask your surgeon when your vision is stable enough for a glasses prescription. Your eyes keep settling for a while after surgery: before a second laser treatment, for example, the FDA advises waiting until your measurements are the same at two visits at least three months apart.",
  "tp.lasik.q4": "Do I need glasses for night driving after LASIK?",
  "tp.lasik.a4": "Only if your eye doctor says so. Some people see glare or halos at night after LASIK, and the AAO notes you might need glasses for activities such as driving at night. If your doctor writes a prescription for it, we make the lenses, with an anti-reflective coating if you want one.",
  "tp.contacts-vs.meta": "Contacts vs Glasses: Pros, Cons, Cost | House of Eyeglasses",
  "tp.contacts-vs.h1": "Contact lenses vs glasses: pros, cons and cost",
  "tp.contacts-vs.lead": "Glasses are simpler: nothing touches your eye, there is little upkeep, and you pay once until your prescription changes. Contact lenses give you clear side vision with nothing on your face, but they need their own fitting and prescription, daily care and a steady supply of new lenses. Many people use both. We make glasses; we don’t sell contact lenses today.",
  "tp.contacts-vs.r1h": "Why people choose glasses",
  "tp.contacts-vs.r1p": "<p>Nothing touches your eye. There is no lens to clean, store or replace, and no harm done if you fall asleep in them. They go on and off in a second.</p><p>A frame is also part of how you look: shape, color and size are yours to choose. And the lenses can do more than correct your vision: an <a href=\"anti-glare-glasses.html\">anti-reflective coating</a>, <a href=\"photochromic-glasses.html\">light-responsive lenses</a> that darken outdoors, or a sun tint for <a href=\"sunglasses.html\">prescription sunglasses</a>. Strong prescriptions stay thin with <a href=\"high-index-lenses.html\">high-index lenses</a>.</p>",
  "tp.contacts-vs.r2h": "Why people choose contacts",
  "tp.contacts-vs.r2p": "<p>Contacts move with your eye, so no frame cuts into your side vision, and nothing fogs up or slides down your nose. Many people prefer them for sports, and they don’t change how your face looks.</p>",
  "tp.contacts-vs.r3h": "What contacts ask of you",
  "tp.contacts-vs.r3p": "<p>A contact lens prescription is separate from a glasses prescription. It is written after a contact lens fitting, which can take more than one visit, and it names the lens (its manufacturer or brand), its base curve and, when needed, its diameter. A glasses prescription has none of that, so one can’t be used for the other. Under the Federal Trade Commission’s Contact Lens Rule, a contact lens prescription is valid for at least one year unless your prescriber has a medical reason to make it shorter.</p><p>Contacts are medical devices that need a prescription and daily care. The FDA advises against sleeping in daily wear lenses and warns of infection from bacteria in pools, hot tubs, lakes and the ocean; the CDC says sleeping in daily wear lenses can increase the chance of a serious eye infection. The American Academy of Ophthalmology suggests seeing an eye specialist every year if you wear contacts, and the FDA recommends always having a backup pair of glasses with a current prescription.</p>",
  "tp.contacts-vs.r4h": "What each one costs",
  "tp.contacts-vs.r4p1": "Glasses are mostly a one-time cost: a frame and lenses made to your prescription, kept until your prescription changes. Contacts are a running cost: the CDC describes daily disposables, a new pair every day, and planned-replacement lenses changed every 7 to 30 days; add solution and a case for the reusable kind, the fitting, and the backup glasses you need anyway. Contact lens prices depend on the brand, the replacement schedule and your prescription, so ask your eye doctor for a yearly estimate and compare it with one pair of glasses (<a href=\"prescription-glasses-cost.html\">what glasses cost here</a>).",
  "tp.contacts-vs.r4x2": "Here, a complete pair of prescription glasses starts at {frame_min+single}: our lowest-priced frame, {frame_min}, with single vision lenses, {single}.",
  "tp.contacts-vs.r5h": "Using both",
  "tp.contacts-vs.r5p": "<p>Many people wear contacts most days and glasses at home, at night, or when their eyes need a break, and the backup pair the FDA recommends is exactly that. Your glasses are made from your glasses prescription, not from the contact lens one, so keep both current. With a current glasses prescription, any frame here can be made for you: <a href=\"receta.html\">check your numbers</a> and <a href=\"catalogo.html\">choose a frame</a>.</p>",
  "tp.contacts-vs.src": "<p><a href=\"https://consumer.ftc.gov/articles/buying-prescription-glasses-or-contact-lenses-your-rights\">Federal Trade Commission (FTC): Buying prescription glasses or contact lenses, your rights</a></p><p><a href=\"https://www.ftc.gov/business-guidance/resources/contact-lens-rule-guide-prescribers-sellers\">Federal Trade Commission: The Contact Lens Rule</a></p><p><a href=\"https://www.fda.gov/medical-devices/contact-lenses/everyday-eye-care\">U.S. Food and Drug Administration: Contact lenses, everyday eye care</a></p><p><a href=\"https://www.cdc.gov/contact-lenses/about/about-contact-lens-types.html\">Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (CDC): Contact lens types</a></p><p><a href=\"https://www.aao.org/eye-health/tips-prevention/eye-exams-101\">American Academy of Ophthalmology: Eye exam and vision testing basics</a></p>",
  "tp.contacts-vs.q1": "Can I use my contact lens prescription to buy glasses?",
  "tp.contacts-vs.a1": "No. They are different prescriptions. For glasses you need a glasses prescription, which your eye doctor must give you, at no extra charge, after an eye exam that includes a refraction.",
  "tp.contacts-vs.q2": "Is it safe to sleep in contact lenses?",
  "tp.contacts-vs.a2": "Not in daily wear lenses: the FDA and the CDC both say it raises the chance of an eye infection. Some lenses are approved for overnight wear, but the CDC links overnight wear with serious eye infections, so follow your eye doctor’s instructions.",
  "tp.contacts-vs.q3": "Which costs less, contacts or glasses?",
  "tp.contacts-vs.a3": "It depends on the lenses and your prescription. Glasses are paid once and kept until your prescription changes; contacts are replaced on a schedule and still need a backup pair of glasses. Compare a year of contacts, with the fitting and care products, against one pair of glasses.",
  "tp.contacts-vs.q4": "Do you sell contact lenses?",
  "tp.contacts-vs.a4": "Not today. We make prescription glasses: designer frames with lenses made to your glasses prescription.",
  "tp.signs.meta": "Signs You Need Glasses | House of Eyeglasses",
  "tp.signs.h1": "Signs you need glasses (and what to do next)",
  "tp.signs.lead": "Blurry vision, far away or up close, is the most common sign. Others are squinting, tired or sore eyes, headaches, glare or halos around lights, and trouble seeing at night. Only an eye exam can tell whether glasses will help, so the next step is an appointment with an optometrist or an ophthalmologist.",
  "tp.signs.r1h": "The common signs",
  "tp.signs.r1p": "<p><strong>Blurry vision.</strong> The National Eye Institute (NEI) calls it the most common symptom of a refractive error, the kind of vision problem glasses correct. Road signs or the TV look soft, or you hold your phone farther away to read it.</p><p><strong>Squinting.</strong> Needing to squint to see clearly is on the NEI’s list for nearsightedness and for astigmatism.</p><p><strong>Eye strain and headaches.</strong> Eyes that feel tired or sore after reading, screens or driving, and headaches, especially when reading.</p><p><strong>Glare, halos and night vision.</strong> Seeing a glare or halo around bright lights, or more trouble seeing at night, which the NEI lists for astigmatism.</p><p><strong>Double or hazy vision.</strong> Both are on the NEI’s list of symptoms of refractive errors.</p>",
  "tp.signs.r2h": "What each sign points to",
  "tp.signs.r2p": "<p>Trouble seeing far away points to nearsightedness. Trouble up close points to farsightedness or, from your 40s, to presbyopia. Blur at every distance can be astigmatism. Many people have more than one, and mild cases may cause no symptoms at all. These are clues, not a diagnosis: <a href=\"nearsighted-vs-farsighted.html\">nearsighted vs farsighted</a> and <a href=\"glasses-for-astigmatism.html\">glasses for astigmatism</a> explain each one.</p>",
  "tp.signs.r3h": "If you already wear glasses",
  "tp.signs.r3p": "<p>The same signs can mean your prescription has changed. The NEI says that if you wear glasses or contact lenses and still have these symptoms, you might need a new prescription. Check the date on your paper too: <a href=\"how-long-is-a-prescription-valid.html\">how long a prescription is valid</a>.</p>",
  "tp.signs.r4h": "Children may not tell you",
  "tp.signs.r4p": "<p>Children often don’t know their vision isn’t normal: the NEI notes they may be less likely to realize it, and the American Academy of Ophthalmology says they are unlikely to complain about blurry vision. The American Optometric Association recommends a child’s eye exam at 6 to 12 months, at least once between ages 3 and 5, and before first grade and every year after that.</p>",
  "tp.signs.r5h": "When not to wait",
  "tp.signs.r5p": "<p>Some changes are an emergency, not a reason to order glasses. According to the NEI, a sudden increase in floaters, flashes of light in one or both eyes, or a shadow or “curtain” over part of your vision can be signs of a detached retina: go to your eye doctor or the emergency room right away.</p>",
  "tp.signs.r6h": "What to do next",
  "tp.signs.r6p": "<p>Book a comprehensive eye exam with an optometrist or an ophthalmologist (a medical doctor who specializes in the eyes). Under the Federal Trade Commission’s Eyeglass Rule, after an exam that includes a refraction they must give you a copy of your glasses prescription, whether you ask for it or not, and you don’t have to buy your glasses from them.</p><p>With the prescription in hand, <a href=\"receta.html\">learn to read it</a>, then <a href=\"catalogo.html\">choose a frame</a>: we make the lenses with your numbers, as <a href=\"single-vision-glasses.html\">single vision</a> or <a href=\"progressive-glasses.html\">progressive</a> lenses.</p>",
  "tp.signs.src": "<p><a href=\"https://www.nei.nih.gov/eye-health-information/eye-conditions-and-diseases/refractive-errors\">National Eye Institute (NEI): Refractive errors</a></p><p><a href=\"https://www.nei.nih.gov/eye-health-information/eye-conditions-and-diseases/astigmatism\">National Eye Institute: Astigmatism</a></p><p><a href=\"https://www.nei.nih.gov/eye-health-information/eye-conditions-and-diseases/retinal-detachment\">National Eye Institute: Retinal detachment</a></p><p><a href=\"https://www.aao.org/eye-health/astigmatism\">American Academy of Ophthalmology: Astigmatism</a></p><p><a href=\"https://www.aao.org/eye-health/tips-prevention/what-is-ophthalmologist\">American Academy of Ophthalmology: Ophthalmologist vs optometrist</a></p><p><a href=\"https://www.aoa.org/healthy-eyes/caring-for-your-eyes/eye-exams\">American Optometric Association (AOA): Comprehensive eye exams</a></p><p><a href=\"https://consumer.ftc.gov/articles/buying-prescription-glasses-or-contact-lenses-your-rights\">Federal Trade Commission (FTC): Buying prescription glasses or contact lenses, your rights</a></p>",
  "tp.signs.q1": "Can I tell if I need glasses without an eye exam?",
  "tp.signs.a1": "You can notice the signs, but not their cause or your prescription, and mild problems may have no signs at all. A vision screening, like the one at school or for a driver’s license, can’t replace a comprehensive eye exam, the American Optometric Association says.",
  "tp.signs.q2": "Do headaches mean I need glasses?",
  "tp.signs.a2": "They can. The NEI lists headaches and eye strain among the symptoms of refractive errors, but headaches have many other causes. An eye exam tells whether your eyes are part of it.",
  "tp.signs.q3": "Can I just buy reading glasses at the store?",
  "tp.signs.a3": "Reading glasses can be bought without a prescription, but the American Academy of Ophthalmology says the power you need should be determined by an eye exam. Store readers have the same power in both eyes and no astigmatism correction.",
  "tp.signs.q4": "How often should I get my eyes checked?",
  "tp.signs.a4": "It depends on your age and risk. The American Optometric Association recommends an exam at least every two years from 18 to 64 and every year from 65. <a href=\"how-often-eye-exam.html\">How often should you get an eye exam?</a> has both major schedules.",
  "tp.rx-valid.meta": "How Long Is an Eyeglass Prescription Valid?",
  "tp.rx-valid.h1": "How long is an eyeglass prescription valid?",
  "tp.rx-valid.lead": "Usually one or two years from your eye exam. In the United States, state law decides when a glasses prescription expires, and your eye doctor writes the date on it; the Federal Trade Commission (FTC) says glasses prescriptions are usually good for a year or two, but state laws vary. The date on your paper is the one that counts.",
  "tp.rx-valid.r1h": "Where to find the date",
  "tp.rx-valid.r1p": "<p>Many prescriptions show the exam date and an expiration date, often near the doctor’s signature. If yours shows only the exam date, ask the office that wrote it how long it is valid in your state. <a href=\"receta.html\">How to read your prescription</a> explains every other number on the paper.</p>",
  "tp.rx-valid.r2h": "Why prescriptions expire",
  "tp.rx-valid.r2p": "<p>Eyes change. Nearsightedness usually starts between ages 6 and 14 and gets worse until the early twenties, and presbyopia gets worse over time until it usually stops after 65, the National Eye Institute says; astigmatism can change gradually too, according to the American Academy of Ophthalmology. An exam also checks the health of your eyes, not just the numbers: many eye and vision problems have no obvious signs, the American Optometric Association notes.</p>",
  "tp.rx-valid.r3h": "Your right to a copy",
  "tp.rx-valid.r3p": "<p>Under the FTC’s Eyeglass Rule, your eye doctor must give you a copy of your glasses prescription immediately after an eye exam that includes a refraction, whether you ask for it or not, at no extra charge. They can’t require you to buy glasses from them, and they can’t ask you to sign a waiver or liability release to get it. They may offer a digital copy, but you have the right to a paper one, and if they sell glasses they must ask you to confirm that you received it.</p><p>If you think a prescriber is breaking the rule, the FTC asks you to report it at ReportFraud.ftc.gov.</p>",
  "tp.rx-valid.r4h": "The PD may not be on it",
  "tp.rx-valid.r4p": "<p>Your pupillary distance (PD) tells the lab where to center each lens. The FTC notes that most states do not require it on the prescription, and that in some states the prescriber can charge for measuring it. If yours doesn’t have it, <a href=\"receta.html#pd\">measure your PD at home</a> with a ruler, or ask your eye doctor.</p>",
  "tp.rx-valid.r5h": "Contact lens prescriptions are different",
  "tp.rx-valid.r5p": "<p>A contact lens prescription is valid for at least one year unless your prescriber has a medical reason to make it shorter (FTC). It is a separate prescription, written after a contact lens fitting, and it can’t be used to make glasses. More in <a href=\"contacts-vs-glasses.html\">contact lenses vs glasses</a>.</p>",
  "tp.rx-valid.r6h": "Ordering with us",
  "tp.rx-valid.r6p": "<p>We make lenses only from a current prescription that has not expired. If yours has expired, see an eye doctor for a new exam first; we don’t do eye exams. Then type your numbers or upload a photo of the prescription in the lens step of any <a href=\"catalogo.html\">frame</a>.</p>",
  "tp.rx-valid.src": "<p><a href=\"https://consumer.ftc.gov/articles/buying-prescription-glasses-or-contact-lenses-your-rights\">Federal Trade Commission (FTC): Buying prescription glasses or contact lenses, your rights</a></p><p><a href=\"https://www.ftc.gov/business-guidance/resources/contact-lens-rule-guide-prescribers-sellers\">Federal Trade Commission: The Contact Lens Rule</a></p><p><a href=\"https://www.nei.nih.gov/eye-health-information/eye-conditions-and-diseases/nearsightedness-myopia\">National Eye Institute: Nearsightedness (myopia)</a></p><p><a href=\"https://www.nei.nih.gov/eye-health-information/eye-conditions-and-diseases/presbyopia\">National Eye Institute: Presbyopia</a></p><p><a href=\"https://www.aao.org/eye-health/astigmatism\">American Academy of Ophthalmology: Astigmatism</a></p><p><a href=\"https://www.aoa.org/healthy-eyes/caring-for-your-eyes/eye-exams\">American Optometric Association (AOA): Comprehensive eye exams</a></p>",
  "tp.rx-valid.q1": "Can I order glasses with an expired prescription?",
  "tp.rx-valid.a1": "Not here. We make lenses only from a current prescription. If yours has expired, get a new eye exam: your vision may have changed since the last one.",
  "tp.rx-valid.q2": "Does my eye doctor have to give me my prescription?",
  "tp.rx-valid.a2": "Yes. Under the FTC’s Eyeglass Rule, after an eye exam that includes a refraction, your prescriber must give you a copy at no extra charge, even if you don’t ask. They can ask you to pay for the exam first only if they ask that of every patient.",
  "tp.rx-valid.q3": "What if my prescription has no expiration date?",
  "tp.rx-valid.a3": "Ask the office that wrote it how long it is valid in your state. The FTC notes that glasses prescriptions are usually good for a year or two, but state laws vary.",
  "tp.rx-valid.q4": "Is a contact lens prescription the same as a glasses prescription?",
  "tp.rx-valid.a4": "No. A contact lens prescription names the lens manufacturer or brand, its base curve and, when needed, its diameter, and it is valid for at least a year unless there is a medical reason for less. Glasses need their own prescription.",
  "tp.astig.meta": "Glasses for Astigmatism | House of Eyeglasses",
  "tp.astig.h1": "Glasses for astigmatism: lenses and frames that work",
  "tp.astig.lead": "Regular prescription glasses correct astigmatism. The CYL and AXIS numbers on your prescription tell the lab how much correction to add and in which direction, and any single vision or progressive lens can carry it. What matters most is an exact prescription, a frame that fits and sits level, and a thin lens if your numbers are high.",
  "tp.astig.r1h": "What astigmatism is",
  "tp.astig.r1p": "<p>Astigmatism happens when the cornea (the clear front of the eye) or the lens inside it has a different shape than normal, the National Eye Institute (NEI) explains: more like a football than a basketball, in the American Academy of Ophthalmology’s words. Light doesn’t focus evenly, so things look blurry or distorted, near and far. Other common symptoms are squinting, eye strain, headaches and trouble seeing at night.</p><p>It is very common. The AAO says most people have 0.5 to 0.75 diopters of astigmatism, and people with 1.5 or more typically need glasses or contacts to see clearly. Only an eye exam can tell how much you have.</p>",
  "tp.astig.r2h": "The numbers: CYL and AXIS",
  "tp.astig.r2p": "<p><strong>CYL</strong> (cylinder) is the amount of astigmatism correction, in diopters. Blank or 0.00 means none. Doctors may write it with a minus or a plus sign: both describe the same correction, and the lab works with either.</p><p><strong>AXIS</strong> is the direction of that correction, a number from 1 to 180 degrees. It is a direction, not a strength: 180 is not stronger than 90. It only appears next to a CYL value.</p><p>Example: OD −1.50 −0.75 × 180 means SPH −1.50, CYL −0.75, AXIS 180. <a href=\"receta.html\">How to read your prescription</a> has a free checker that spells out every sign.</p>",
  "tp.astig.r3h": "Which lenses",
  "tp.astig.r3p1": "<a href=\"single-vision-glasses.html\">Single vision lenses</a> if your prescription has no ADD; <a href=\"progressive-glasses.html\">progressive lenses</a> if it has one and you want far and near in one pair. Both carry your CYL and AXIS.",
  "tp.astig.r3p2": "CYL adds power in one direction, so it counts toward how thick the lens gets: the material we recommend in the last step adds SPH and CYL together. Up to about ±4 in total, the polycarbonate included with every pair is thin enough; from about ±4, <a href=\"high-index-lenses.html\">high-index 1.67 or 1.74</a> keeps the lens thin. An <a href=\"anti-glare-glasses.html\">anti-reflective coating</a> takes reflections off the lens surfaces.",
  "tp.astig.r3x3": "High Index 1.67: {index_167} · High Index 1.74: {index_174} · anti-reflective coating: {anti_reflective}, on top of your lenses.",
  "tp.astig.r4h": "Frames that work",
  "tp.astig.r4p": "<p>Any frame can take astigmatism lenses; fit matters more than style. Because the correction has a direction, the lab sets it at your AXIS, and a frame that sits level and doesn’t slip keeps it where your eye doctor measured it. Choose one that rests evenly on your nose and ears, about as wide as your face, with your eyes near the center of each lens: <a href=\"talla.html\">find your frame size</a> with the numbers on the glasses you own.</p><p>With a strong prescription, a smaller, rounder lens and a full rim keep the thick edges less visible.</p>",
  "tp.astig.r5h": "New glasses and night driving",
  "tp.astig.r5p": "<p>A new or changed prescription can take a little getting used to. The AAO notes that mild eye strain, headaches or a sense of blur can happen with a new prescription, particularly in the first week; if they last longer, have the prescription rechecked.</p><p>Trouble seeing at night is one of the symptoms of astigmatism (NEI). The right prescription is what corrects it; an anti-reflective coating helps by keeping the lenses themselves from adding reflections from headlights and street lamps.</p>",
  "tp.astig.src": "<p><a href=\"https://www.nei.nih.gov/eye-health-information/eye-conditions-and-diseases/astigmatism\">National Eye Institute: Astigmatism</a></p><p><a href=\"https://www.aao.org/eye-health/astigmatism\">American Academy of Ophthalmology: Astigmatism</a></p><p><a href=\"https://www.aao.org/eye-health/ask-ophthalmologist-q/new-glasses-prescription-headache-eye-strain\">American Academy of Ophthalmology: Headaches or eye strain with a new prescription</a></p><p><a href=\"https://www.nei.nih.gov/eye-health-information/eye-conditions-and-diseases/refractive-errors\">National Eye Institute (NEI): Refractive errors</a></p>",
  "tp.astig.q1": "Can glasses correct astigmatism?",
  "tp.astig.a1": "Yes. Glasses and contact lenses are the most common treatments for astigmatism, the National Eye Institute says. For severe or irregular astigmatism, the AAO notes rigid gas permeable contact lenses may work best; your eye doctor will tell you what suits your eyes.",
  "tp.astig.q2": "Do I need special frames for astigmatism?",
  "tp.astig.a2": "No. Any frame we carry can take astigmatism lenses. What matters is a good fit: a frame that sits level and stays in place, so the correction stays where it was measured.",
  "tp.astig.q3": "Is astigmatism the same as nearsightedness?",
  "tp.astig.a3": "No, though they often come together. Nearsightedness blurs things far away; astigmatism can blur near and far. One prescription corrects both: SPH for nearsightedness or farsightedness, CYL and AXIS for astigmatism.",
  "tp.astig.q4": "Do high-index lenses work with astigmatism?",
  "tp.astig.a4": "Yes. 1.67 and 1.74 carry your CYL and AXIS like any other lens. They are worth it when SPH and CYL together go past about ±4.",
  "tp.near-far.meta": "Nearsighted vs Farsighted | House of Eyeglasses",
  "tp.near-far.h1": "Nearsighted vs farsighted: what your prescription means",
  "tp.near-far.lead": "Look at the sign in front of the SPH number. A minus (−) means nearsighted: far things look blurry. A plus (+) means farsighted: close things look blurry. The bigger the number, the stronger the lens. An ADD is extra power for reading, common after 40, and it is not the same as being farsighted.",
  "tp.near-far.r1h": "Nearsighted (myopia)",
  "tp.near-far.r1p": "<p>Far-away objects look blurry because the shape of the eye makes light focus in front of the retina instead of on it, the National Eye Institute (NEI) explains. Other signs are squinting and eye strain. It usually starts between ages 6 and 14 and gets worse until the early twenties.</p><p>On a prescription: a minus SPH, like −1.25 or −4.00. The lens is thinnest in the center and thicker at the edge.</p>",
  "tp.near-far.r2h": "Farsighted (hyperopia)",
  "tp.near-far.r2p": "<p>Nearby objects look blurry because light focuses behind the retina, when the eyeball is too short from front to back or the cornea or the lens has a different shape (NEI). Common symptoms are trouble seeing up close, eye strain and headaches, especially when reading. Mild farsightedness may cause no symptoms at all.</p><p>On a prescription: a plus SPH, like +1.00 or +3.25. The lens is thicker in the center.</p>",
  "tp.near-far.r3h": "Presbyopia and the ADD",
  "tp.near-far.r3p": "<p>Presbyopia is a normal part of aging, not farsightedness: the lens inside the eye gets harder and less flexible and stops focusing up close (NEI). You may start to notice it shortly after 40, the American Academy of Ophthalmology says, often by holding your phone or a menu farther away. Everyone gets it with age, whether nearsighted, farsighted or neither.</p><p>On a prescription it appears as an ADD: a plus number, usually between +0.75 and +3.00, often written once for both eyes. With an ADD, <a href=\"progressive-glasses.html\">progressive lenses</a> give you far and near in one pair; for reading only, your eye doctor can write a near prescription for <a href=\"single-vision-glasses.html\">single vision lenses</a>.</p>",
  "tp.near-far.r4h": "Reading real examples",
  "tp.near-far.r4p": "<p><strong>OD −2.25 · OS −2.00:</strong> both eyes nearsighted, the right one a little more.</p><p><strong>OD +1.50 · OS +1.75:</strong> both eyes farsighted.</p><p><strong>OD −0.75 · OS +0.50:</strong> one eye of each kind. One pair of glasses corrects both.</p><p><strong>SPH −3.00 · CYL −1.00 · AXIS 90 · ADD +2.00:</strong> nearsighted, with astigmatism and a reading addition: a typical prescription for progressive lenses.</p><p><strong>PL or plano:</strong> 0.00, no nearsighted or farsighted correction in that eye.</p>",
  "tp.near-far.r5h": "Which lenses",
  "tp.near-far.r5p": "<p>No ADD: <a href=\"single-vision-glasses.html\">single vision lenses</a>, one power for the distance your prescription is written for. With an ADD: <a href=\"progressive-glasses.html\">progressive lenses</a>, or office lenses for long hours at a desk. Strong prescriptions on either side of zero stay thin with <a href=\"high-index-lenses.html\">high-index lenses</a>. Not sure you copied yours right? <a href=\"receta.html\">Check your numbers and their signs</a> before you order.</p>",
  "tp.near-far.src": "<p><a href=\"https://www.nei.nih.gov/eye-health-information/eye-conditions-and-diseases/nearsightedness-myopia\">National Eye Institute: Nearsightedness (myopia)</a></p><p><a href=\"https://www.nei.nih.gov/eye-health-information/eye-conditions-and-diseases/farsightedness-hyperopia\">National Eye Institute: Farsightedness (hyperopia)</a></p><p><a href=\"https://www.nei.nih.gov/eye-health-information/eye-conditions-and-diseases/presbyopia\">National Eye Institute: Presbyopia</a></p><p><a href=\"https://www.aao.org/eye-health/diseases/what-is-presbyopia\">American Academy of Ophthalmology: What is presbyopia?</a></p><p><a href=\"https://www.aao.org/eye-health/astigmatism\">American Academy of Ophthalmology: Astigmatism</a></p>",
  "tp.near-far.q1": "Is −2.00 nearsighted or farsighted?",
  "tp.near-far.a1": "Nearsighted. Any minus SPH is nearsighted and any plus SPH is farsighted; the size of the number is the strength of the lens.",
  "tp.near-far.q2": "Can one eye be nearsighted and the other farsighted?",
  "tp.near-far.a2": "Yes. Each eye has its own numbers, and one pair of glasses corrects both. More often, a nearsighted person also gets presbyopia after 40 and needs help far and near: that is what the ADD and progressive lenses are for.",
  "tp.near-far.q3": "What does ADD mean on a prescription?",
  "tp.near-far.a3": "Extra magnifying power for reading. It is always a plus number, usually between +0.75 and +3.00, often written once for both eyes, and common after 40.",
  "tp.near-far.q4": "Do nearsighted and farsighted lenses look different?",
  "tp.near-far.a4": "Yes. A minus lens is thinnest in the center and thicker at the edge; a plus lens is thicker in the center. With a strong prescription, high-index lenses and a smaller frame keep either one thinner.",
  "tp.cost.meta": "How Much Do Prescription Glasses Cost? | House of Eyeglasses",
  "tp.cost.h1": "How much do prescription glasses cost online?",
  "tp.cost.lead": "Here, a pair costs the price of the frame you choose plus the lenses made to your prescription. Single vision lenses cost the least; progressive lenses, high-index materials and coatings add to the total. Standard U.S. shipping is free, and you see the full price before you pay.",
  "tp.cost.price": "Today: frames from {frame_min} · single vision lenses {single} · a complete pair from {frame_min+single}.",
  "tp.cost.r1h": "How the price adds up",
  "tp.cost.r1p1": "<strong>The frame.</strong> Every frame shows its own price on its page.",
  "tp.cost.r1p2": "<strong>The lens type.</strong> <a href=\"single-vision-glasses.html\">Single vision</a>, <a href=\"progressive-glasses.html\">progressive</a> (Standard, Premium or Ultimate) or <a href=\"computer-glasses.html\">office</a> lenses.",
  "tp.cost.r1p3": "<strong>The material.</strong> <a href=\"polycarbonate-lenses.html\">Polycarbonate</a> is included. <a href=\"high-index-lenses.html\">High Index 1.67 or 1.74</a> is an upgrade for strong prescriptions.",
  "tp.cost.r1p4": "<strong>Coatings and filters.</strong> Optional: <a href=\"anti-glare-glasses.html\">anti-reflective</a>, scratch-resistant, <a href=\"blue-light-glasses.html\">blue-light filter</a>, <a href=\"photochromic-glasses.html\">light-responsive lenses</a> or a <a href=\"sunglasses.html\">sun tint</a>.",
  "tp.cost.r1x5": "Lens prices today: single vision {single} · progressive {estandar}, {premium} or {ultimate} · office {office} · High Index 1.67 {index_167} · High Index 1.74 {index_174} · anti-reflective {anti_reflective} · scratch-resistant {scratch} · blue-light filter {blue_light} · light-responsive {light_responsive} · sun tint {tint}.",
  "tp.cost.r2h": "Three example totals",
  "tp.cost.r2p1": "Each example uses the lowest-priced frame in our catalog today. Another frame changes only the frame part of the total.",
  "tp.cost.r2x2": "<strong>Basic single vision:</strong> frame {frame_min} + single vision lenses {single} = {frame_min+single}.",
  "tp.cost.r2x3": "<strong>Progressive:</strong> frame {frame_min} + Standard progressive lenses {estandar} = {frame_min+estandar}.",
  "tp.cost.r2x4": "<strong>High-index single vision:</strong> frame {frame_min} + single vision lenses {single} + High Index 1.67 {index_167} + anti-reflective coating {anti_reflective} = {frame_min+single+index_167+anti_reflective}.",
  "tp.cost.r3h": "What’s included",
  "tp.cost.r3p": "<p>Lenses made to your prescription and PD, in polycarbonate unless you choose high-index. Free standard shipping in the U.S., with an estimated delivery of 5–10 business days; prescription lenses are made to order, so they also need lab time. If we make an error filling your prescription, we remake the lenses at no cost (<a href=\"legal.html#returns\">returns policy</a>). Prices are in U.S. dollars; sales tax, where it applies, is calculated at checkout.</p>",
  "tp.cost.r4h": "Ways to spend less",
  "tp.cost.r4p": "<p>Choose single vision if your prescription has no ADD. Keep polycarbonate up to about ±4: it is included, and high-index would cost more without a difference you could see. Add only the coatings you will use.</p><p>Many vision plans reimburse glasses bought out of network: check your benefits, order, and send the itemized receipt with your plan’s claim form. Prescription eyeglasses are generally an eligible expense for FSA and HSA accounts; ask us on WhatsApp whether your card works for your order. Details in <a href=\"ayuda.html#insurance\">Help</a>.</p>",
  "tp.cost.r5h": "What you need to order",
  "tp.cost.r5p": "<p>A current prescription from your eye doctor and your pupillary distance (PD). We don’t do eye exams. If your prescription has expired, see <a href=\"how-long-is-a-prescription-valid.html\">how long a prescription is valid</a>; if the PD isn’t on it, <a href=\"receta.html#pd\">measure it at home</a>.</p>",
  "tp.cost.q1": "Is the price on a frame’s page the full price?",
  "tp.cost.a1": "It is the price of the frame. The lenses are added in the lens step, and you see the full total before you pay.",
  "tp.cost.q2": "Why do progressive lenses cost more?",
  "tp.cost.a2": "They do the work of distance and reading glasses in one lens. We make them in three levels, Standard, Premium and Ultimate; the price of each is on the <a href=\"progressive-glasses.html\">progressive glasses</a> page and in the lens step.",
  "tp.cost.q3": "Is shipping free?",
  "tp.cost.a3": "Standard shipping within the U.S. is free. The estimated delivery time is 5–10 business days, and prescription lenses also need lab time before they ship.",
  "tp.cost.q4": "Can I use vision insurance or FSA/HSA?",
  "tp.cost.a4": "Many vision plans reimburse glasses bought out of network: send your itemized receipt to your insurer. Prescription eyeglasses are generally an eligible FSA and HSA expense; ask us on WhatsApp whether your card works for your order.",
  "tp.exam.meta": "How Often Should You Get an Eye Exam? | House of Eyeglasses",
  "tp.exam.h1": "How often should you get an eye exam?",
  "tp.exam.lead": "It depends on your age and your risk. The American Optometric Association (AOA) recommends an exam at least every two years from 18 to 64, every year from 65, and at least yearly for anyone at higher risk. The American Academy of Ophthalmology (AAO) schedule for adults without risk factors is every 5 to 10 years before 40, then more often with age, up to every 1 to 2 years from 65. Your eye doctor can tell you which fits you.",
  "tp.exam.r1h": "Adults: the AOA schedule",
  "tp.exam.r1p": "<p><strong>18 to 39:</strong> at least every two years.</p><p><strong>40 to 64:</strong> at least every two years.</p><p><strong>65 and older:</strong> every year.</p><p><strong>At risk, at any age:</strong> at least once a year, or as your eye doctor recommends.</p><p>The AOA counts as at risk, among others, people with a personal or family history of eye disease, health conditions that can affect the eyes, medicines with side effects on the eyes, sight in only one eye, contact lenses, eye surgery or a past eye injury, a high or changing prescription, or work that is visually demanding or risky for the eyes. It also lists belonging to certain racial and ethnic groups.</p>",
  "tp.exam.r2h": "Adults: the AAO schedule",
  "tp.exam.r2p": "<p>For adults with no symptoms and no risk factors for eye disease, the AAO’s 2025 guideline recommends a comprehensive eye exam:</p><p><strong>Under 40:</strong> every 5 to 10 years.</p><p><strong>40 to 54:</strong> every 2 to 4 years.</p><p><strong>55 to 64:</strong> every 1 to 3 years.</p><p><strong>65 and older:</strong> every 1 to 2 years.</p><p>The AAO also recommends a baseline eye disease screening by age 40, because early signs of disease and changes in vision often begin at that age.</p>",
  "tp.exam.r3h": "Children",
  "tp.exam.r3p": "<p>The AOA recommends an eye exam at 6 to 12 months, at least once between ages 3 and 5, and before first grade and every year after that, more often for children at risk. The AAO and the American Association for Pediatric Ophthalmology and Strabismus publish their own childhood screening guidelines. A school vision screening helps, but the AOA notes that it can’t replace a comprehensive exam.</p>",
  "tp.exam.r4h": "When to go more often",
  "tp.exam.r4p": "<p><strong>Diabetes.</strong> The AAO recommends a first eye exam at diagnosis for type 2 diabetes and five years after onset for type 1, then at least once a year.</p><p><strong>Contact lenses.</strong> The AAO says to see an eye specialist every year.</p><p><strong>After LASIK, PRK or SMILE.</strong> The AOA recommends an eye exam every 1–2 years (see <a href=\"glasses-after-lasik.html\">glasses after LASIK</a>).</p><p><strong>A family history of eye disease.</strong> Ask your eye doctor how often to be examined.</p>",
  "tp.exam.r5h": "Don’t wait for the date",
  "tp.exam.r5p": "<p>Go sooner if your vision changes: blurry vision, squinting, eye strain, headaches or trouble seeing at night are common <a href=\"signs-you-need-glasses.html\">signs you may need glasses</a> or a new prescription. A sudden increase in floaters, flashes of light or a shadow over part of your vision is an emergency: the National Eye Institute says to go to your eye doctor or the emergency room right away.</p>",
  "tp.exam.r6h": "After the exam",
  "tp.exam.r6p": "<p>If the exam includes a refraction, the Federal Trade Commission’s Eyeglass Rule says your eye doctor must give you a copy of your glasses prescription right away, at no extra charge, and you can buy your glasses wherever you like. It is usually valid for a year or two, depending on your state: see <a href=\"how-long-is-a-prescription-valid.html\">how long a prescription is valid</a>. Then <a href=\"receta.html\">read your prescription</a> and <a href=\"catalogo.html\">choose a frame</a>; we make the lenses with your numbers. We don’t do eye exams ourselves.</p>",
  "tp.exam.src": "<p><a href=\"https://www.aoa.org/healthy-eyes/caring-for-your-eyes/eye-exams\">American Optometric Association (AOA): Comprehensive eye exams</a></p><p><a href=\"https://www.aao.org/education/preferred-practice-pattern/comprehensive-adult-medical-eye-evaluation-ppp\">American Academy of Ophthalmology: Comprehensive Adult Medical Eye Evaluation, Preferred Practice Pattern (2025)</a></p><p><a href=\"https://www.aao.org/eye-health/tips-prevention/screening\">American Academy of Ophthalmology: Get an eye disease screening by age 40</a></p><p><a href=\"https://www.aao.org/eye-health/tips-prevention/eye-exams-101\">American Academy of Ophthalmology: Eye exam and vision testing basics</a></p><p><a href=\"https://www.nei.nih.gov/eye-health-information/eye-conditions-and-diseases/retinal-detachment\">National Eye Institute: Retinal detachment</a></p><p><a href=\"https://consumer.ftc.gov/articles/buying-prescription-glasses-or-contact-lenses-your-rights\">Federal Trade Commission (FTC): Buying prescription glasses or contact lenses, your rights</a></p>",
  "tp.exam.q1": "Is a vision screening the same as an eye exam?",
  "tp.exam.a1": "No. Screenings at school, at the doctor’s office or for a driver’s license can find some problems, but the AOA says they can’t replace a comprehensive eye exam, the only effective way to confirm or rule out an eye disease or vision problem.",
  "tp.exam.q2": "How often should I get an eye exam if I wear contacts?",
  "tp.exam.a2": "Every year. The AAO says contact lens wearers should see an eye specialist yearly, and the AOA counts them among the patients who need an exam at least once a year.",
  "tp.exam.q3": "How often should someone with diabetes get an eye exam?",
  "tp.exam.a3": "At least once a year. The AAO recommends the first exam at diagnosis for type 2 diabetes and five years after onset for type 1, then yearly; pregnancy changes the schedule, so ask your eye doctor.",
  "tp.exam.q4": "Does an eye exam include a glasses prescription?",
  "tp.exam.a4": "If it includes a refraction, the test that measures your prescription, yes: under the FTC’s Eyeglass Rule your eye doctor must give you a copy right away, at no extra charge, whether you ask or not.",
  "receta.docTitle": "How to Read an Eyeglass Prescription | House of Eyeglasses",
  "rx2.h1": "How to read your eyeglass prescription: what the numbers mean",
  "rx2.kicker": "Every abbreviation",
  "rx2.lead": "How to read an eyeglass prescription, line by line: what each letter and number means.",
  "rx2.t.od.h": "OD: right eye",
  "rx2.t.od.p": "<p>OD is short for oculus dexter, Latin for right eye. Everything on the OD line is for your right eye. Some prescriptions write RE or R instead.</p>",
  "rx2.t.os.h": "OS: left eye",
  "rx2.t.os.p": "<p>OS is short for oculus sinister, the left eye. Some prescriptions write LE or L, and prescriptions in Spanish often write OI (ojo izquierdo).</p>",
  "rx2.t.ou.h": "OU: both eyes",
  "rx2.t.ou.p": "<p>OU (oculus uterque) means both eyes. A value written once next to OU, often the ADD, is the same for the right and the left eye.</p>",
  "rx2.t.sph.h": "SPH: sphere",
  "rx2.t.sph.p": "<p>The main power of the lens, in diopters. A minus sign (−) corrects nearsightedness: far is blurry. A plus sign (+) corrects farsightedness. The bigger the number, the stronger the lens; PL or plano means zero. More in <a href=\"nearsighted-vs-farsighted.html\">nearsighted vs farsighted</a>.</p>",
  "rx2.t.cyl.h": "CYL: cylinder",
  "rx2.t.cyl.p": "<p>Extra power for astigmatism, in diopters, when the eye is shaped a little more like a football than a basketball. Blank or 0.00 means no astigmatism. It can be written with a minus or a plus sign; both are valid. See <a href=\"glasses-for-astigmatism.html\">glasses for astigmatism</a>.</p>",
  "rx2.t.axis.h": "AXIS: direction",
  "rx2.t.axis.p": "<p>The direction of the astigmatism correction, from 1 to 180 degrees. It only appears with a CYL value, and it is a direction, not a strength: 180 is not stronger than 90. Many prescriptions write it after an ×, as in −0.75 × 180.</p>",
  "rx2.t.add.h": "ADD: reading addition",
  "rx2.t.add.p": "<p>Extra magnifying power for close-up, always a plus number, usually between +0.75 and +3.00. It is common after 40 and often written once for both eyes. With an ADD, <a href=\"progressive-glasses.html\">progressive lenses</a> give you far and near in one pair.</p>",
  "rx2.t.prism.h": "PRISM: prism and base",
  "rx2.t.prism.p": "<p>A correction that shifts the image, for eyes that don’t line up and see double. It comes with a base direction: BU (up), BD (down), BI (in) or BO (out). Most prescriptions leave it blank. Our lens step has no prism field, so if yours has one, contact us before you order.</p>",
  "rx2.t.pd.h": "PD: pupillary distance",
  "rx2.pdCam": "Prefer your camera? <a href=\"pd.html\">Measure your PD online</a>: it takes about a minute, and no photo leaves your device.",
  "rx2.t.pd.p": "<p>The distance between the centers of your pupils, in millimeters, used to center each lens in front of your eye. It can be one number (63) or two, one per eye (31.5/31.5). Many prescriptions leave it out: <a href=\"#pd\">measure it at home</a> with a ruler, or <a href=\"pd.html\">online with your camera</a>.</p>",
  "rx2.t.exp.h": "Expiration date",
  "rx2.t.exp.p": "<p>Glasses prescriptions expire, usually one or two years after the exam, depending on your state and your doctor. We can only make lenses from a prescription that has not expired. See <a href=\"how-long-is-a-prescription-valid.html\">how long a prescription is valid</a>.</p>",
  "gd.ayuda.prescription-valid.q": "How long is an eyeglass prescription valid?",
  "gd.ayuda.prescription-valid.a": "Usually one or two years from the exam, depending on your state and your eye doctor; the date is on the prescription. Your rights and the contact lens rules: <a href=\"how-long-is-a-prescription-valid.html\">How long is an eyeglass prescription valid?</a>",
  "gd.ayuda.eye-exam-often.q": "How often should I get an eye exam?",
  "gd.ayuda.eye-exam-often.a": "It depends on your age and risk: the American Optometric Association recommends at least every two years from 18 to 64 and every year from 65. We don’t do eye exams; the full schedules are in <a href=\"how-often-eye-exam.html\">How often should you get an eye exam?</a>",
  "gd.ayuda.astigmatism.q": "What glasses are best for astigmatism?",
  "gd.ayuda.astigmatism.a": "Any frame and any of our lenses can carry your CYL and AXIS. A good fit and, with a strong prescription, high-index lenses matter most: <a href=\"glasses-for-astigmatism.html\">Glasses for astigmatism</a>.",
  "gd.ayuda.glasses-cost.q": "How much do prescription glasses cost?",
  "gd.ayuda.glasses-cost.a": "The price of the frame plus the lenses you choose; you see the full total before you pay. Today’s lens prices and three example totals: <a href=\"prescription-glasses-cost.html\">How much do prescription glasses cost online?</a>",
});
